Subject: wasix: serve a level-triggered socket from the waiting thread

A guest blocked in epoll_wait on a socket is woken twice over: the
kernel wakes the shared selector's poll thread, which dispatches into
the set and then wakes the guest thread. This is the first user of the
direct descriptor wait: the thread that waits on the set polls the
sockets' descriptors itself, and the selector is not involved.

A level-triggered subscription on a socket with a host descriptor is
claimed by `claim_direct_serving` when it is registered: it takes its
place in the epoll registry but installs no handler on the socket.
`direct_epoll_wait` polls that descriptor through
`try_suspend_on_pollfd` and queues what the kernel reported, exactly as
the selector's dispatch would have; a guest that does not drain its
socket is told again on the next call, which is what level triggering
means. Edge-triggered and one-shot subscriptions, sockets without a
descriptor, pipes and event notifications register and report exactly as
before, and a set that mixes the two waits on both.

The existing blocking wait is never entered while a direct-served
subscription exists, because nothing would be watching its socket. Every
way out of the direct loop other than answering the call demotes the set
first (`demote_direct` installs the skipped registrations), and a thread
entering the existing path counts itself in with `enter_blocking` and
demotes what it finds; a subscription registered while that count is not
zero is not claimed. The count is held by the wait future, so a deep
sleep does not release it early, and a call resumed from one answers
with its result before looking at the set. A demotion that cannot
install its registration reports the subscription broken rather than
leaving a wait to sleep on it.

The loop demotes and falls through when the runtime has no descriptor
wait, the call is journalled, the deadline overflows, the set exceeds
`DIRECT_POLL_CAP` (64), a socket can no longer be named, a signal cannot
be delivered, the wait answers unavailable for a reason other than a
fork, or a thread is already in the existing path. A fork is not a
failure: `POLLFD_EPOCH` says so, and the loop resolves its descriptors
again.

A number is not an identity. A subscription is polled on the socket it
named when it was claimed; `produce` queues readiness only while that
subscription is still the set's for the number; and a demotion attaches
it only to that socket, reporting it broken otherwise. A turn compares
the set's `direct_generation` once it is registered to be woken, so a
change another waiter was notified of is not slept through, and asks
`should_exit` after capturing its interrupt generation, because a
termination moves that generation without queueing a signal.

A zero-timeout call is answered by the loop rather than passed on, so a
non-blocking call can see a socket nothing else is watching. Every turn
runs the pending dynamic link operations first, as the existing path
does. One trace event per call, a debug event per demotion and per
claim. The loop's working arrays live on the frame, sized by
`DIRECT_POLL_CAP`, the notifier future is pinned there, and the waker is
built once per call, so a turn allocates only the events it returns.

